1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,280
Today is November 06, 2026.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:15,295
Overnight, Wall Street suffered a brutal, one-way selloff, and the Nasdaq 100 plunged 6.36 percent.

4
00:00:15,295 --> 00:00:23,199
The S&P 500 fell 4.36 percent, or 335 points, and closed at its lows.

5
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:30,282
The Nasdaq Composite lost 5.52 percent and sliced through its 250-day moving average.

6
00:00:30,282 --> 00:00:35,417
It also broke its July 29 low, which confirms a large double top.

7
00:00:35,417 --> 00:00:42,709
The Dow opened higher, then collapsed 1,833 points, or 3.44 percent.

8
00:00:42,709 --> 00:00:47,795
The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index crashed 12.25 percent.

9
00:00:47,795 --> 00:00:51,152
That was its worst day since March 2020.

10
00:00:51,152 --> 00:00:53,628
Fear came back in force.

11
00:00:53,628 --> 00:00:58,444
The VIX spiked as much as 70 percent intraday and settled near 28.

12
00:00:58,444 --> 00:01:03,567
The CNN Fear and Greed Index dropped to 20, deep in extreme fear.

13
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:06,888
In bonds, money fled into Treasuries.

14
00:01:06,888 --> 00:01:09,902
Prices rallied and yields moved lower.

15
00:01:09,902 --> 00:01:13,579
Oil and yields had already eased before the crash.

16
00:01:13,579 --> 00:01:15,981
This was not a rates shock.

17
00:01:15,981 --> 00:01:21,459
On the Fed, markets want to know if officials will soften their tone after this turmoil.

18
00:01:21,459 --> 00:01:26,851
Morgan Stanley warns that rates staying high will still cap growth stock valuations.

19
00:01:26,851 --> 00:01:31,532
The upcoming nonfarm payrolls report is the next big macro test.

20
00:01:31,532 --> 00:01:35,724
Overseas, Korea's KOSPI triggered another circuit breaker.

21
00:01:35,724 --> 00:01:39,645
Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix were hammered.

22
00:01:39,645 --> 00:01:43,775
Seoul and New York dragged each other lower in a feedback loop.

23
00:01:43,775 --> 00:01:46,018
The trigger was political.

24
00:01:46,018 --> 00:01:49,951
Democrats won both chambers of Congress in the midterms.

25
00:01:49,951 --> 00:01:56,655
Traders fear tighter data center permits, antitrust pressure, and curbs on frontier AI research.

26
00:01:56,655 --> 00:02:03,983
Goldman Sachs estimates that about 45 percent of planned US data center capacity faces scrutiny or bans.

27
00:02:03,983 --> 00:02:09,682
Senator Sanders' September proposal to ban superintelligence came back as a fear point.

28
00:02:09,682 --> 00:02:12,599
The bears see a structural repricing.

29
00:02:12,599 --> 00:02:18,150
Citi says to cut earnings estimates across AI hardware and avoid bottom fishing.

30
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:23,996
Mike Wilson at Morgan Stanley expects more choppy downside before a cleaner entry window.

31
00:02:23,996 --> 00:02:26,373
The bulls push back hard.

32
00:02:26,373 --> 00:02:32,513
Under a divided government, sweeping federal data center limits are nearly impossible for two years.

33
00:02:32,513 --> 00:02:35,233
The White House can simply veto them.

34
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:40,785
Goldman stresses that forward valuations sit far below the peaks of the 2000 bubble.

35
00:02:40,785 --> 00:02:45,809
In its view, this is valuation compression, not the start of a bear market.

36
00:02:45,809 --> 00:02:48,456
Positioning favors a rebound.

37
00:02:48,456 --> 00:02:53,652
CTA equity exposure sits at the 2nd percentile of its one-year range.

38
00:02:53,652 --> 00:02:56,692
Hedge fund net exposure is also low.

39
00:02:56,692 --> 00:02:59,326
That is fuel for short covering.

40
00:02:59,326 --> 00:03:01,851
But history says be patient.

41
00:03:01,851 --> 00:03:09,731
Across 48 prior Nasdaq drops of 5 percent or more, the median next-day gain was just 0.3 percent.

42
00:03:09,731 --> 00:03:13,738
Only 54 percent of those next days closed higher.

43
00:03:13,738 --> 00:03:18,934
The selling hit hardest where leverage was most crowded, in AI infrastructure.

44
00:03:18,934 --> 00:03:24,290
Margin calls and quant stop losses fed each other in a forced deleveraging spiral.

45
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:30,932
The triple-leveraged chip fund, S O X L, lost 38.7 percent in one day.

46
00:03:30,932 --> 00:03:33,444
The chain reaction is simple.

47
00:03:33,444 --> 00:03:36,643
Slower permits mean later data centers.

48
00:03:36,643 --> 00:03:40,368
That means later hardware deliveries and later revenue.

49
00:03:40,368 --> 00:03:44,682
Chip stocks were priced for years of nonstop capex growth.

50
00:03:44,682 --> 00:03:47,758
Even one soft quarter breaks that story.

51
00:03:47,758 --> 00:03:52,966
Nvidia has now lost roughly 1 trillion dollars in market value from its peak.

52
00:03:52,966 --> 00:04:00,074
Even if hyperscalers absorb every GPU, their growing bargaining power threatens Nvidia's premium margins.

53
00:04:00,074 --> 00:04:03,787
Qualcomm's weak guidance the night before added fuel.

54
00:04:03,787 --> 00:04:06,079
Memory took a direct hit.

55
00:04:06,079 --> 00:04:11,373
Micron fell about 17 percent, slipping below 700 dollars.

56
00:04:11,373 --> 00:04:17,415
SanDisk plunged 22 percent and now sits 65 percent below its June peak.

57
00:04:17,415 --> 00:04:21,349
JPMorgan argues memory fundamentals are still tight.

58
00:04:21,349 --> 00:04:25,576
DRAM prices keep rising and HBM is even scarcer.

59
00:04:25,576 --> 00:04:30,282
The market, however, is already trading as if the cycle has peaked.

60
00:04:30,282 --> 00:04:33,371
The relative winners are the cloud giants.

61
00:04:33,371 --> 00:04:39,192
Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, and Meta fell far less than hardware names.

62
00:04:39,192 --> 00:04:45,760
Microsoft jumped 6.68 percent the day before, then dropped 3.58 percent.

63
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:48,260
It is still up since the election.

64
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:53,052
The logic is that slower new builds make existing compute more valuable.

65
00:04:53,052 --> 00:04:56,581
Hardware and financing costs should ease too.

66
00:04:56,672 --> 00:05:00,310
The real danger sits with debt-funded neoclouds.

67
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:06,179
When projects slip, the interest, rent, and GPU depreciation keep coming.

68
00:05:06,179 --> 00:05:13,924
Microsoft is reportedly tightening oversight of neocloud projects, including Nebius, IREN, and Nscale.

69
00:05:13,924 --> 00:05:17,491
The chip index has broken below 10,000.

70
00:05:17,491 --> 00:05:22,736
Its next major support is near 8,300, its prior record high.

71
00:05:22,736 --> 00:05:25,652
Now to today's actionable trades.

72
00:05:25,652 --> 00:05:28,410
There are two buys and two sells.

73
00:05:28,410 --> 00:05:35,469
Buy number one is broad index exposure through S P Y and Q Q Q, not single chip stocks.

74
00:05:35,469 --> 00:05:41,951
Indexes usually recover faster than individual names, and year-end seasonality offers support.

75
00:05:41,951 --> 00:05:48,863
On the S&P 500, the first buy zone is 7,300 to 7,320.

76
00:05:48,863 --> 00:05:51,706
That is the floor of the prior range.

77
00:05:51,706 --> 00:05:58,116
The final line of defense is 7,224 to 7,245.

78
00:05:58,116 --> 00:06:03,618
That band covers the 250-day average and a major JPMorgan put strike.

79
00:06:03,618 --> 00:06:09,991
If the index flushes into that zone, holds, and reverses sharply, that would confirm a bottom.

80
00:06:09,991 --> 00:06:15,211
A decisive break below 7,224 cancels the setup.

81
00:06:15,211 --> 00:06:17,711
Cut exposure if it happens.

82
00:06:17,711 --> 00:06:22,797
For Q Q Q, 700 is the line between bulls and bears.

83
00:06:22,797 --> 00:06:27,025
Below 700, every bounce is only a relief rally.

84
00:06:27,025 --> 00:06:31,253
Take profits into strength at 680 and 700.

85
00:06:31,253 --> 00:06:36,486
In an extreme case, the Nasdaq 100 could revisit 26,000.

86
00:06:36,486 --> 00:06:39,231
Buy number two is A M D.

87
00:06:39,231 --> 00:06:43,986
The stock held firm after earnings, then got dragged into the selloff.

88
00:06:43,986 --> 00:06:49,329
Its 250-day moving average sits near 376 dollars.

89
00:06:49,329 --> 00:06:53,348
That is the key defense, and a rebound is expected there.

90
00:06:53,348 --> 00:06:56,572
Scale in near 376.

91
00:06:56,572 --> 00:07:01,621
A decisive close below 376 means the trend is broken.

92
00:07:01,621 --> 00:07:03,790
Exit if that happens.

93
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:07,429
Take partial profits into the first sharp rebound.

94
00:07:07,429 --> 00:07:09,966
Sell number one is CoreWeave.

95
00:07:09,966 --> 00:07:12,613
It gapped down and kept falling.

96
00:07:12,613 --> 00:07:17,956
About 40 percent of its planned data centers are in states like Ohio and Texas.

97
00:07:17,956 --> 00:07:21,412
Permitting risk is rising fast in those states.

98
00:07:21,412 --> 00:07:26,081
Its interest costs are fixed while its revenue keeps getting pushed back.

99
00:07:26,081 --> 00:07:29,268
Use any relief bounce to cut or exit.

100
00:07:29,268 --> 00:07:31,964
Sell number two is Teradyne.

101
00:07:31,964 --> 00:07:36,130
It broke down on heavy volume and shattered its technical support.

102
00:07:36,130 --> 00:07:40,174
Even a short-term bounce is unlikely to restore the uptrend.

103
00:07:40,174 --> 00:07:43,692
Sell into rallies, and do not average down.

104
00:07:43,692 --> 00:07:51,682
On risk, avoid crowded leveraged trades like S O X L. Build positions in stages, because aftershocks are likely.

105
00:07:51,682 --> 00:07:56,584
Healthcare and renewables could benefit from Democratic policy priorities.

106
00:07:56,584 --> 00:08:06,167
There are four catalysts to watch: nonfarm payrolls, Fed speakers, the scope of state data center bills, and hyperscaler capex guidance.

107
00:08:06,167 --> 00:08:10,174
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

108
00:08:10,174 --> 00:08:14,341
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
